@book{sutton2018rl,
  author    = {Sutton, Richard S. and Barto, Andrew G.},
  title     = {Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction},
  edition   = {2nd},
  publisher = {MIT Press},
  address   = {Cambridge, MA},
  year      = {2018},
  isbn      = {978-0-262-03924-6},
  url       = {http://incompleteideas.net/book/the-book-2nd.html},
  annote    = {Verified 2026-10-09. Opened the official page and the full PDF (RLbook2020.pdf); TOC and body checked.
               Cite: UCB s2.7 Eq. 2.10; contextual bandits s2.9; Bellman eq. for v_pi Eq. 3.14 (s3.5); optimality Eqs. 3.19-3.20 (s3.6);
               DP s4.1-4.4 (policy evaluation update Eq. 4.5); off-policy IS s5.5; per-decision IS s5.9 and s7.4;
               TD(0) s6.1; Sarsa s6.4; Q-learning s6.5; Cliff Walking = Example 6.6; double learning s6.7; n-step Ch. 7;
               semi-gradient s9.3-9.4, s10.1-10.2, s11.1; Baird's counterexample = s11.2, Figure 11.1 (NOT a numbered Example;
               Example 11.1 is Tsitsiklis and Van Roy's); deadly triad s11.3; PG theorem s13.2 Eq. 13.5; REINFORCE s13.3 (Eq. 13.8);
               with baseline s13.4 (Eqs. 13.10-13.11); actor-critic s13.5.
               CAVEAT: the https URL fails (self-signed certificate), so use http. The MIT Press page returned 403, so the series name is unconfirmed.
               Also opened (academic director, Module 0, 2026-10-10, RLbook2020.pdf): s3.1 and Figure 3.1 (the agent-environment interaction);
               s3.3 Eq. 3.7 (G_t = R_{t+1} + R_{t+2} + ... + R_T, the undiscounted return of an episodic task, T the final time step);
               Example 3.4 Pole-Balancing (book p. 56): +1 for every time step on which failure did not occur, so the return is the number of
               steps until failure. Module 0 cites these three.
               Also opened (academic director, Module 2, 2026-10-17, RLbook2020.pdf from the official page, text extracted with pypdf): s4.2 Policy
               Improvement (pp. 76-79): the policy improvement theorem, Eqs. 4.7-4.8 (p. 78), the greedy policy Eq. 4.9 (p. 79); s4.3 Policy Iteration
               (p. 80); s4.4 Value Iteration, update Eq. 4.10 (p. 83); s4.5 Asynchronous Dynamic Programming (p. 85); s4.6 Generalized Policy Iteration
               (p. 86); s4.8 bibliographical remarks attribute the policy improvement theorem and policy iteration to Bellman (1957a) and Howard (1960)
               (neither opened). Module 2 cites s4.2-4.6.
               Also opened (academic director, Module 2 third pass, 2026-10-17, same PDF): s4.3, Exercise 4.4 (book p. 82, PDF p. 104): the policy iteration
               pseudocode 'may never terminate if the policy continually switches between two or more policies that are equally good'.
               Module 2 cites it for the tie rule's tolerance.
               Same page, s4.3: 'Policy iteration often converges in surprisingly few iterations'; Module 2 cites it in s3.
               Also opened (academic director, Module 3, 2026-10-17, RLbook2020.pdf, text extracted from the downloaded file):
               s5.1 (first-visit and every-visit MC defined; both converge, every-visit credited to Singh and Sutton 1996);
               s6.1 Eq. 6.1 (constant-alpha MC), Eq. 6.2 (TD(0)), Eq. 6.5 (TD error), the TD(0) box (V(terminal) = 0);
               s6.2 (TD usually found to converge faster than constant-alpha MC on stochastic tasks; no general proof);
               s6.4 Eq. 6.7 (Sarsa; the quintuple gives the name; Q(S_{t+1}, A_{t+1}) = 0 if terminal); s6.5 Eq. 6.8 (Q-learning,
               Watkins 1989; the box initializes Q(terminal, .) = 0); Example 6.6 (undiscounted cliff, epsilon = 0.1: Q-learning
               learns the optimal-policy values and its online performance is worse than Sarsa's; with epsilon decayed both converge);
               s7.1 Eq. 7.1 (n-step return; G_{t:t+n} = G_t when t + n >= T), Eq. 7.2 (n-step TD), Eq. 7.3 (error reduction),
               Figure 7.2 (19-state random walk, values initialized to 0, error averaged over the first 10 episodes and 100 runs:
               an intermediate n works best); convergence conditions (Module 3 sign-off, 2026-10-17): s6.4, Sarsa converges w.p. 1 to an
               optimal policy and Q under the usual step-size conditions if all pairs are visited infinitely often and the policy
               becomes greedy in the limit (e.g. epsilon = 1/t); s6.5, Q-learning converges w.p. 1 to q* if all pairs keep being updated,
               under a variant of the usual step-size conditions;
               the s6.1 text places TD(lambda) in Chapter 12; the Chapter 6 bibliographical notes (book p. 139): Sarsa introduced by
               Rummery and Niranjan (1994) as Modified Connectionist Q-learning, the name Sarsa introduced by Sutton (1996).
               Also opened (academic director, Module 4, 2026-10-18, RLbook2020.pdf, text extracted): s2.7 (book pp. 35-36): the UCB rule
               A_t = argmax_a [Q_t(a) + c sqrt(ln t / N_t(a))], Eq. 2.10, with c > 0 controlling exploration and an action with N_t(a) = 0
               treated as maximizing; Figure 2.4 compares it with epsilon-greedy on the 10-armed testbed. Module 4 cites s2.7, Eq. 2.10.
               Also opened (academic director, Module 5, 2026-10-17, RLbook2020.pdf): s2.9 Associative Search (Contextual Bandits) (book p. 41): such tasks
               'are often now called contextual bandits'; they are intermediate between the k-armed bandit and the full problem; 'each action affects only
               the immediate reward. If actions are allowed to affect the next situation as well as the reward, then we have the full reinforcement
               learning problem.' Module 5 cites s2.9.
               Also opened (academic director, Module 6, 2026-10-18, same PDF, pp. 34-35): s2.6 Optimistic Initial Values: optimistic initial estimates
               make even greedy methods explore; "a simple trick that can be quite effective on stationary problems, but it is far from being a generally
               useful approach to encouraging exploration"; not well suited to nonstationary problems because its drive for exploration is temporary.}
}

