               s4.9 (p. 68): Thompson [1933] is the earliest reference; s5.3 Definition 5.2, Theorem 5.3 (p. 77), Lemma 5.4, Corollary 5.5 and
               Eqs. 5.6-5.7 (p. 78), Example 5.6(c): X in [a, b] with mean zero is (b - a)/2-subgaussian; s5.4 Note 4 (p. 80): Hoeffding's lemma
               and Hoeffding's inequality, Eq. 5.8, P((1/n) sum (X_t - E X_t) >= eps) <= exp(-2 n^2 eps^2 / sum (b_t - a_t)^2), for independent
               X_t in [a_t, b_t]; Exercise 5.11; Chapter 6 introduction (p. 91): the subgaussian constant is fixed to 1, results hold for other
               constants by scaling the rewards, and 'All the algorithms that follow rely on the knowledge of sigma'; s7.1 Eqs. 7.1-7.3,
               Algorithm 3 UCB(delta) (p. 103; index infinite for an arm never played), Theorem 7.1 (delta = 1/n^2, 1-subgaussian, p. 105);
               s7.3 (p. 111): Auer et al.'s UCB1 uses t rather than n in the confidence level and assumes pay-offs in [0, 1], which are
               1/2-subgaussian; s34.3 Example 34.6 (p. 430): Beta(alpha, beta) prior with Bernoulli rewards gives the posterior
               Beta(alpha + S_t, beta + t - S_t); Example 34.7 (p. 430): prior N(mu, sigma^2) with rewards N(theta, 1) gives the posterior
               N(mu_t, sigma_t^2), mu_t = (mu/sigma^2 + S_t)/(1/sigma^2 + t), sigma_t^2 = (1/sigma^2 + t)^(-1); Chapter 36 introduction (p. 460):
