               p. 461); s36.5 Note 1 (p. 471): three equivalent views of TS; Note 2: asymptotically optimal for single-parameter exponential
               families with a suitable prior (Kaufmann et al. 2012, Korda et al. 2013, not opened), and not a silver bullet; Note 5 (p. 472):
               the prior matters, and a prior that underestimates an arm may mean it is never played. Module 4 cites these.
               Also opened (academic director, Module 5, 2026-10-17, book.pdf from the URL above, 597 PDF pages, text extracted with pypdf; book page numbers):
               s19.2 (pp. 240-242): the stochastic linear bandit and its random pseudo-regret; regularized least squares, Eq. 19.4, its solution, Eq. 19.5,
               V_0 = lambda I and V_t, Eq. 19.6; 'Choosing lambda > 0 helps because it ensures that the loss function has a unique minimiser even when
               A_1, ..., A_t do not span R^d'; the ellipsoid ||theta - theta_hat_{t-1}||^2_{V_{t-1}} <= beta_t, Eq. 19.7. s19.3 (pp. 243-245): Assumption 19.1,
               Theorem 19.2, Eq. 19.8, Corollary 19.3 (R_n <= C d sqrt(n) log(nL)), and Eq. 19.13, the action argmax <theta_hat, a> + sqrt(beta_t) ||a||_{V^-1}.
               Ch. 20 opening (pp. 254-256): confidence sets for bandit data are 'a surprisingly delicate matter'; with lambda = 0, independent 1-subgaussian
