             "Training algorithm for deep Q-networks": experience replay, target network cloned every C updates, TD error clipped to [-1, 1] in the update;
             Algorithm 1. CAVEAT: the paper never says "Huber". Pages say: clipping delta in the gradient of (1/2)delta^2 gives exactly the gradient of the
             Huber loss with threshold 1. The gradients are equal, not the loss values. Clipping the LOSS is not equivalent.
             Also opened (academic director, Module 8, 2026-10-24): the published letter with Methods and Extended Data, as hosted by DeepMind
             (storage.googleapis.com/deepmind-media/dqn/DQNNaturePaper.pdf; "RESEARCH LETTER", page footers 529 and 530), text extracted with pypdf.
             p. 529: RL with a nonlinear approximator "is known to be unstable or even to diverge"; three causes: correlations in the sequence of
             observations, small updates to Q that change the policy and the data distribution, and correlations between Q and the target values;
             experience replay "randomizes over the data", the target values are "only periodically updated". Methods, "Training algorithm for deep
             Q-networks": replay gives data efficiency (each step "potentially used in many weight updates"), breaks correlations and "therefore
             reduces the variance of the updates", and averages the behaviour distribution, avoiding feedback loops; learning by replay must be
             off-policy, "which motivates the choice of Q-learning"; only the last N tuples are stored and sampled uniformly at random, and
             prioritized sampling is mentioned as a possible refinement; "every C updates we clone the network Q to obtain a target network",
             which makes "divergence or oscillations much more unlikely"; the Methods' loss section calls the result "a sequence of well-defined
             optimization problems"; quote: "We also found it helpful to clip the error term from the update ... to be between -1 and 1" and
             "corresponds to using an absolute value loss function for errors outside of the (-1,1) interval" (the paper's words are "clipping the
             squared error"; the paper never says "Huber"); RMSProp is the optimizer. Algorithm 1: y_j = r_j if the episode terminates at step
             j+1, else r_j + gamma max_a' Q-hat(phi_{j+1}, a'; theta^-); "Every C steps reset Q-hat = Q". Module 8 cites p. 529, Methods and Algorithm 1.}
