                  Practical Advice; under Practical Advice: Work from a reference implementation; Assume you have a bug; Loss curves are a red
                  herring; Unit test the tricky bits; Use probe environments.; Use probe agents.; Use adaptive network definitions; Log
                  excessively.; also "Hand-tune your reward scale". Quoted on the Module 9 page: probes 1-3 specs ("One action, zero observation,
                  one timestep long, +1 reward every timestep"; "One action, random +1/-1 observation, one timestep long, obs-dependent +1/-1 reward
                  every time"; "One action, zero-then-one observation, two timesteps long, +1 reward at the end"); "This isolates the value
                  network."; "backpropagation through my network is broken"; probe 3 failing means "my reward discounting is broken" (paraphrased
                  on the page); "it should only take a second or two to learn them"; "Most often, it turns out they've got a bug."; "The shape of
                  your loss curve says very little about where in your code you've messed up"; "Actually, anything [-.1, +.1]ish to [-10, +10]ish
                  is good." The post says it comes from the author's experience and from months of helping people on the RL Discord. Its "Log
                  excessively" list includes policy entropy, KL, residual variance, terminal correlation, value-target, reward, value, advantage
                  and episode-length distributions, step and gradient statistics.}
}

